%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b9a8d65017e7c2d408570e6a83a8662a, type: 3}
  m_Name: TutorKnowledgeBase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Project8.AI.TutorKnowledgeBase
  entries:
  - keywords:
    - black hole
    - binary
    - merger
    - chirp
    - inspiral
    - orbit
    answer: 'The two dark spheres with glowing orange accretion discs are a black-hole binary. They orbit faster and faster as they spiral inward, then merge - the flash marks coalescence, when the emitted strain peaks: a ''chirp'' signal that sweeps upward in frequency and amplitude, like GW150914.'
  - keywords:
    - net
    - lattice
    - web
    - lines
    - grid
    - sheet
    - cloth
    answer: 'The translucent sheet is an analogy for spacetime: heavy objects dent it, and the passing wave ripples across it. The deformation is hugely exaggerated - the real stretching of space is far too small to see.'
  - keywords:
    - einstein
    - telescope
    - 'et '
    - triangle
    - detector
    - interferometer
    - ligo
    answer: 'The triangular facility at the end of the mission is the Einstein Telescope model: three interferometers in an equilateral triangle, each station''s arms at 60 degrees, and each station nesting a low- and a high-frequency instrument - the real 2020 design baseline. Like LIGO''s Michelsons it splits laser light, sends it down two arms and recombines it at the splitter; a wave shifts each round trip differently so the dark ports read differently. Our model shows the principle only - no cavities, suspensions or noise curves.'
  - keywords:
    - lf
    - hf
    - low frequency
    - high frequency
    - lane
    - xylophone
    answer: 'Each ET corner station actually hosts TWO interferometers: one cryogenic low-frequency instrument and one high-frequency instrument - the ''xylophone'' layout. In this app the two lanes are drawn separately but share one simplified strain response; the real instruments cover different frequency bands with different noise curves.'
  - keywords:
    - strain
    - delta l
    - relative change
    answer: Strain h = deltaL / L is the relative change in separation between two free test masses. If they are 1 m apart and the wave changes the distance by 0.01 m, the strain is 0.01. Real detector strain is around 1e-21.
  - keywords:
    - stretch
    - squeeze
    - polarization
    - ellipse
    - deform
    answer: A passing gravitational wave alternately stretches space in one transverse direction and squeezes it in the perpendicular one - the '+' polarization. Watch the sheet ripple orange (stretch) and blue (squeeze) as the ring expands past each station.
  - keywords:
    - wavefront
    - marker
    - reached
    - ahead
    - behind
    answer: The wavefront is the expanding bright ring on the sheet. Stations at different distances from the merger see it at different times - that ordering is what the prediction task asks about.
  - keywords:
    - speed
    - slow
    - light
    - fast
    - velocity
    answer: Real gravitational waves travel at the speed of light - you could never watch or surf one. This app only slows the VISUALIZATION speed; the model itself is unchanged. That is a viewing choice, not a physical claim.
  - keywords:
    - exaggerat
    - scale
    - real
    - small
    - actually
    answer: Real GW strain on Earth is about 1e-21 - utterly invisible. Following LIGO's public visualization practice, the deformation shown here is enormously exaggerated so the pattern can be seen at all.
  - keywords:
    - ride
    - surf
    - perspective
    - view
    - frame
    - camera
    answer: Riding alongside the wave is a pedagogical viewpoint, not a real relativistic observer frame. It lets you inspect propagation; the physics parameters stay the same - only your viewpoint changes.
  - keywords:
    - frequency
    - pitch
    - cycles
    answer: Frequency f sets how many stretch-and-squeeze cycles occur per second of simulation time. Raise it and the ring oscillates faster between ellipses.
  - keywords:
    - amplitude
    - h0
    - strength
    - how big
    answer: h0 is the strain amplitude - the maximum relative deformation. Bigger h0 means a deeper stretch and a harder squeeze as the wave passes.
  - keywords:
    - invariant
    - same
    - change
    - compare
    answer: When you switch viewpoints, the propagation direction, frequency and amplitude stay the same - they are model parameters. What changes is only your reference frame, a visualization choice.
  - keywords:
    - mass
    - particle
    - ring
    - object
    - sphere
    - grid
    answer: 'Free test masses are what a detector measures: the wave changes their separations, and that relative change is all a detector can see. Here the ET stations'' mirrors play that role - each arm endpoint is a test mass.'
  - keywords:
    - pause
    - replay
    - predict
    - task
    - reset
    answer: Pause mid-flight and predict which ET station the wavefront reaches next - D1, D2 or D3. Replay starts the wave over.
  - keywords:
    - direction
    - propagat
    - travel
    answer: The wave spreads outward from the merger in every direction. Watch the order in which the three stations - at different distances - respond.
  fallbackAnswer: Good question. In this visualization a simplified gravitational wave passes a field of test masses. Try asking about strain, stretch and squeeze, the wavefront markers, why the motion is slowed down, or which values are exaggerated.
